WEBVTT

00:00.500 --> 00:07.849
PhysRL3D: Reinforcement Post-Training of Image-to-3D Generators with Physical Rewards for Real-World Fabrication.

00:09.300 --> 00:11.794
A generated object should do more than look right.

00:11.794 --> 00:18.055
For real-world fabrication, it should stand, use material efficiently, and preserve the intended geometry.

00:19.350 --> 00:27.659
PhysRL3D uses DiffusionNFT to post-train the generator with simulator-free rewards, encouraging standability in the generated pose.

00:31.300 --> 00:34.118
A centre-of-mass margin rewards static balance.

00:34.118 --> 00:39.218
No simulator is queried during post-training; these simulations evaluate the outputs.

00:39.566 --> 00:41.848
Here are the final states at ten seconds.

00:43.350 --> 00:45.331
Next, remove the blue supports.

00:45.331 --> 00:50.375
Compare the total material and the percentage used for supports, estimated by the slicer.

00:55.350 --> 00:56.717
Now, beyond the screen.

00:56.717 --> 00:59.172
View the real prints from multiple angles,

01:05.350 --> 01:08.614
then watch what happens when they are released after support removal.

01:15.450 --> 01:23.078
Our real-print examples show that PhysRL3D can improve standability while reducing support-material use and retaining the intended geometry.

01:23.078 --> 01:26.080
This means less material spent on disposable supports.

01:26.080 --> 01:28.402
See the paper for detailed comparisons.

01:31.350 --> 01:32.717
How do we achieve this?

01:32.717 --> 01:39.737
PhysRL3D post-trains the generator with rewards for standability, material efficiency, and geometric fidelity.

01:39.737 --> 01:45.641
The coarse occupancy grid lets us compute the physical rewards without simulator or slicer queries during training.

01:47.350 --> 01:50.324
These objectives can conflict, so their balance matters.

01:50.800 --> 01:57.066
Our Standability-Aware Multi-Reward Scheduling, or SAS, initially prioritises standability.

01:58.550 --> 02:03.801
As standability improves, SAS gives more weight to fidelity and material efficiency.

02:09.050 --> 02:13.910
Their relative shares adapt to progress, rather than following a fixed weighting throughout training.

02:19.350 --> 02:25.817
The result is more standable outputs and lower average slicer-estimated material use, with no added inference cost.

02:27.450 --> 02:35.173
This is PhysRL3D. Reinforcement Post-Training of Image-to-3D Generators
with Physical Rewards for Real-World Fabrication.
